Jenkins is an open-source automation tool used in DevOps. It helps developers automatically build, test, and deploy applications. Jenkins mainly works for automation in the software development process so that manual work becomes less and work becomes faster and more reliable.

Jenkins is mostly used to implement CI/CD pipelines. It connects different tools like Git, Docker, Maven, Terraform, and cloud platforms to automate the workflow.

CI means Continuous Integration. It means developers continuously add their code to a shared repository like GitHub. Jenkins automatically checks that code and builds it.

CD means Continuous Delivery or Continuous Deployment. After building and testing the code, Jenkins can automatically deploy the application to servers.

Jenkins works on a server. Developers push code to a repository such as GitHub or Git and Jenkins detects the changes and starts the automation process.

Jenkins architecture

Jenkins mainly works with two components.

Master (Controller)
Agent (Node)

The master is the main Jenkins server. It manages jobs, schedules builds, and controls the whole automation process.

Agents are machines that perform the actual tasks such as building code, testing applications, or deploying software. Jenkins can use multiple agents to run jobs faster.

Why we need Jenkins

Manual build and deployment take a lot of time and can cause mistakes. Jenkins automates these processes.

Benefits of Jenkins

Automation of build and deployment
Faster software delivery
Early detection of bugs
Integration with many DevOps tools
Easy to scale with multiple agents

Important Jenkins concepts

Job

A job is a task Jenkins performs. For example building code, running tests, or deploying an application.

Build

Build means compiling the source code and creating a runnable version of the application.

Pipeline

A pipeline is a sequence of steps that defines the entire CI/CD process. For example build → test → package → deploy.

Plugins

Jenkins uses plugins to integrate with other tools. Jenkins has thousands of plugins.

Example plugins

Git plugin for code integration
Docker plugin for container builds
Maven plugin for Java builds
Kubernetes plugin for container orchestration

Types of Jenkins jobs

Freestyle project

This is the simplest Jenkins job. It allows you to configure tasks using the Jenkins UI.

Pipeline project

This is the most common in DevOps. It defines the CI/CD pipeline using code.

Multibranch pipeline

This automatically creates pipelines for different branches in Git repositories.

Jenkins pipeline

A pipeline describes the automation steps in code using a file called Jenkinsfile.

The Jenkinsfile is usually stored in the same Git repository as the application code.

Two types of pipelines

Declarative pipeline
Scripted pipeline

Declarative pipeline is simpler and more structured. Scripted pipeline is more flexible but more complex.

Stages in a Jenkins pipeline

A pipeline is divided into stages.

Common stages

Build
Test
Package
Deploy

Example flow

Developer pushes code to GitHub
Jenkins detects the change
Jenkins pulls the code
Jenkins builds the project
Jenkins runs tests
Jenkins deploys the application

Triggers in Jenkins

Triggers decide when Jenkins should start a job.

Common triggers

Git webhook when code is pushed
Scheduled builds using cron
Manual trigger by user

